@section Summary

Tharsis is one Rust codebase that plays the original @emph{Quake} campaign and
large modern community releases such as @emph{Arcane Dimensions}. It runs on
Linux, Windows and macOS, and in a web browser through WebAssembly and WebGPU.
The QuakeC virtual machine, the server and the physics are transliterated by
hand from the GPL source release. A command-driven harness compares them with
the original C engine: discrete events must match exactly, and continuous
values must stay within tolerances frozen before the comparison runs.

The renderer is the showcase. Its shaders are written in Rust with Rust GPU and
built by @code{cargo-gpu}. The same @code{no_std} shading code runs on the GPU,
in CPU unit tests, and in the software reference renderer. Frames are drawn
through wgpu 30. Static world geometry stays on the GPU, a compute pass culls
it and writes indirect draws, and light styles and dynamic lights are evaluated
in shaders instead of by rebuilding lightmaps on the CPU. Everything advanced
is probed at run time and has a simpler equivalent; mesh shaders, ray queries,
path tracing and HDR are optional experiments after the MVP.

The bet on Rust GPU is guarded. M0 ends with a go/no-go gate: the Rust shaders
must compile, translate and draw a real map slice on every MVP platform before
the rest of the plan proceeds. If the gate fails, switching to hand-written
WGSL needs the maintainer's written approval.

