@section Shader pipeline

@itemize
@item
Shading functions live in @code{tharsis-shade}, a @code{no_std} crate:
colormap lighting, light-style blending, dynamic-light falloff, turbulent
warp, sky scroll, fog, gamma and tone mapping. Shader entry points in
@code{tharsis-shaders} call them on the GPU; the software renderer and the
unit tests call them on the CPU. Shared types have explicit size and alignment
tests.
@item
@code{cargo-gpu} compiles the entry points to SPIR-V. naga validates the
SPIR-V and translates it to WGSL for the browser. Metal and DX12 receive naga
translations inside wgpu at pipeline creation.
@item
The generated SPIR-V and WGSL are @strong{committed} under
@file{shaders-out/} with a hash manifest and REUSE coverage. Ordinary builds and
all packages use them on stable Rust and never download or install a nightly
compiler.
@item
A CI job provisions the pinned shader toolchain through a checksum-verified
step, regenerates every artifact offline, and fails if a byte or hash
differs. Toolchain upgrades are separate, reviewed commits.
@end itemize

@section The M0 shader gate

M0 ends with a go/no-go test (R-308, R-309). Before running it, the maintainer
names the reference adapters, operating systems, drivers and browser versions,
and freezes the fixture, sample vectors, tolerances, image thresholds and
expected output. The gate passes only if, on every named target:

@itemize
@item
the Rust vertex, fragment and compute shaders compile, translate and pass naga
and wgpu validation;
@item
a textured, lightmapped BSP fixture draws through an indirect draw; and
@item
the frozen CPU/GPU sample comparisons and image comparisons pass.
@end itemize

The targets are a Linux integrated adapter, a Linux discrete adapter, one
Windows machine, one macOS machine, and one browser. A failed gate blocks M0. A
failure does not pass the gate just because it is documented. The maintainer
may approve, in writing, a narrower platform commitment, a hand-written WGSL
substitution, or a delay; the gate then reruns against the revised scope.

@section Evaluated and rejected

@table @strong
@item Bevy
Not used as the engine. Quake's entity layout, QuakeC order and timing are
compatibility contracts that small purpose-built crates keep visible. This is
an architecture judgment, not a claim that Bevy cannot run Quake.
@item ash (direct Vulkan)
Not the baseline. It would add backend-specific work and break the browser
target. A direct-backend spike needs a measured missing capability.
@item CubeCL
A second GPU runtime is not needed to render Quake. Evaluated after the MVP
for load-time compute only (R-395).
@item Rust CUDA
Single-vendor; never needed to build, play or test.
@item WGSL as the main language
Proposed by the Qwen and Slipgate drafts. Rejected as the default because the
product is a Rust GPU showcase; kept as the gate's approved fallback.
@end table

@node Architecture
@chapter Architecture

@section Porting strategy

The server, the QuakeC VM and the shared physics (@code{sv_*}, @code{pr_*},
@code{world.c}, @code{mathlib.c}, @code{host*.c}, the model loaders) are
@strong{transliterated by hand}. Function boundaries and evaluation order are
kept, so a diff against the GPL source stays readable. Globals and pointers
become owned state, checked indices and explicit lifetimes. The software
renderer (@code{r_*}, @code{d_*}) keeps its algorithms with idiomatic data
structures. The client front end (@code{cl_*}, @code{sbar}, @code{menu},
@code{console}, @code{keys}, @code{cmd}, @code{cvar}) is ported with
idiomatic types. Platform code is new.

@code{c2rust} and a C shim were rejected: both leave C-shaped @code{unsafe}
code or C in the tree, which G3 and NG-6 forbid. No ECS rewrite happens
during the compatibility work.

