Rules, enforced by @code{cargo deny} bans and review:

@itemize
@item
@code{tharsis-sv} and @code{tharsis-qc} depend only on @code{tharsis-math},
@code{tharsis-data} and @code{tharsis-proto}; they never see a renderer.
@item
No renderer depends on @code{tharsis-fs}; renderers receive decoded resources.
@item
Only @code{tharsis-gpu} depends on wgpu. Only @code{tharsis-app} and
@code{tharsis-web} depend on windowing, audio-device or browser crates.
@item
Public interfaces do not expose third-party lock or channel types.
@end itemize

@section State ownership

@itemize
@item
@code{Server} owns the loaded level (BSP, hulls, PVS), the edict arena, the
QuakeC @code{Vm} and the area-node tree. A server frame is a function from
the server state, a fixed time step and ordered client commands to outgoing
messages.
@item
@strong{Edicts} keep vanilla's flat, index-addressed layout, because QuakeC
addresses fields by offset. Every VM field access is bounds-checked against
the entity size the progs declare. Free-list reuse follows vanilla's rules.
@item
Area-node link lists keep vanilla link order, because touch order decides
which trigger fires first.
@item
@code{Client} owns only what the server sent plus interpolation history. A
read-only render snapshot is built from it for each presented frame.
@end itemize

@section QuakeC virtual machine

@itemize
@item
Loads @file{progs.dat} version 6 and checks the header CRC against the
engine's field definitions.
@item
Every instruction checks its global, field, string, function and edict
operands. A bad access ends the call with a run error; partially executed
world changes are never treated as a successful call.
@item
A per-call instruction budget, 100 000 by default and configurable, stops
runaway loops.
@item
File builtins are confined to the active game directory. @code{localcmd} and
@code{stuffcmd} pass through the same command allowlist as user scripts; no
content string reaches a shell or a process.
@item
QuakeSpasm-level extensions needed by the M6 corpus are added only when the
manifest names them (R-311). QSS and FTE extensions are out of scope (NG-2).
@end itemize

@section Server clock

The server ticks at a fixed 72 Hz. Rendering runs uncapped and interpolates
between the two latest server frames. Catch-up work per presented frame is
capped; local play pauses on suspend and the interpolation clock resets on
resume. Slow rendering never changes the simulation step. This is a product
setting, not a claim that every original mode ran at 72 Hz; the fidelity
harness feeds both engines the same explicit time steps (@ref{Quality}).

@section Concurrency model (Standard §3.2)

@multitable @columnfractions .22 .3 .48
@headitem Work @tab Model @tab Decision and trade-off
@item Server frame (QuakeC, physics)
@tab @strong{Serial}, one dedicated server thread
@tab QuakeC order, link order and float evaluation order are game behaviour.
A frame costs about 1 ms or less, so Amdahl's law leaves little to gain and
splitting it would risk fidelity. Serial is chosen and documented here
@item Server and client
@tab Joined by the loopback: bounded SPSC queues natively, @code{postMessage}
between a Web Worker and the page in the browser
@tab The loopback is already the protocol boundary, so a separate thread
costs no fidelity and lets simulation overlap rendering. Reliable messages
are never dropped; a full queue applies back-pressure at a safe point
@item Client and render
@tab Main thread drives the event loop; render snapshots are double-buffered
@tab winit and the browser require the main thread
@item Command encoding
@tab World, model and effect passes on parallel encoders (Rayon), native only
@tab Adopted only if a benchmark beats one encoder without raising frame-time
variance (R-355)
@item GPU culling
@tab Compute pass over all surfaces and entities
@tab Parallel by nature; removes a per-frame CPU loop on Tier 2
@item Level load
@tab Bounded worker pool over lumps, textures, mips, lightmap atlas packing
@tab Kept only if it beats serial on the reference machine. In the browser it
runs serially, because the bundle must not need @code{SharedArrayBuffer}
@item Audio mixing
@tab Audio callback thread fed by a bounded lock-free queue
@tab The client never blocks on audio. On overflow, disposable one-shot
effects are dropped and counted; stop, volume and channel updates are never
dropped. No I/O, locks or allocation in the callback
@item CI corpus
@tab One process per demo, trace or scene
@tab Shared-nothing parallelism
@end multitable

@node Rendering
@chapter Rendering

@section Capability tiers

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .5 .3
@headitem Tier @tab Content @tab Availability
@item Tier 0 — software
@tab Ported WinQuake renderer, presented through softbuffer. Oracle captures
and the no-GPU fallback. Preserves playability, not the GPU performance targets
@tab All platforms
@item Tier 1 — portable GPU
@tab Indexed rasterisation, CPU BSP/PVS and frustum visibility, ordinary
texture bindings, GPU light styles and dynamic lights, GPU model
interpolation, shader sky and liquids. The correctness baseline for every
optimised path
@tab All platforms, including WebGPU
@item Tier 2 — GPU-driven
@tab Compute PVS and frustum culling writing bounded indirect draws; multi-draw
and draw-count where present; binding arrays where present; GPU timestamps;
pipeline caching
@tab Native platforms with full features; on WebGPU with single indirect draws
per batch and no binding arrays
@item Tier 3 — experimental
@tab Mesh shaders, ray queries, path tracing, HDR display output
@tab Optional, after the MVP (M10)
@end multitable

Device creation requests only the features the selected tier uses. Startup
logs the adapter, backend, limits, selected tier and every optional feature it
rejected. A failure in an optional tier falls back one tier; a failure in the
portable path is reported, not hidden.

@strong{Tier 2 adoption gate.} Tier 2 is fully implemented and can always be
forced. It is selected automatically for an adapter class only if it passes
the correctness corpus, improves median frame time by at least 5 percent beyond
run-to-run variance, does not worsen the 99th percentile by more than
2 percent, and stays within resource targets. Otherwise Tier 1 stays the
default and the result is recorded. Small vanilla maps may favour CPU culling.

@section The GPU frame

@itemize
@item
@strong{Static world on the GPU.} Every world surface is triangulated at load
into large vertex and index buffers and one lightmap atlas, uploaded once per
map. Moving brush models are dynamic instances.
@item
@strong{Compute visibility.} The client walks the BSP to the camera leaf and
uploads the PVS as a bitmask. A compute pass tests surface bounds against the
PVS and frustum and writes draw commands. Missing or invalid visibility data
falls back to a conservative visible set; moving doors and teleports never
cause false occlusion.
@item
@strong{Bounded indirect buffers.} Before dispatch, an upper bound on candidate
commands is compared with buffer capacity; if it does not fit, the frame draws
through Tier 1. An overflow on the GPU suppresses the partial result and draws
a complete conservative frame before presentation. A partly culled frame is
never presented as a success.
@item
@strong{Light styles on the GPU.} Each surface keeps up to four style
lightmaps; the 64 style values are a small per-frame array blended in the
fragment shader. The CPU never rebuilds a lightmap when a light flickers.
@item
@strong{Dynamic lights on the GPU.} Muzzle flashes, rockets and explosions are
a storage buffer of lights evaluated with the classic falloff equation and
checked against reference samples.
@item
@strong{Textures.} Binding arrays where supported; batching by material
otherwise. Atlases are an optimisation that needs mip gutters, not a mandatory
fallback.
@item
@strong{Alias models.} Vertex frames live in storage buffers; frame
interpolation and instancing happen in the vertex shader.
@item
@strong{Frame resources.} Per-frame data uses a bounded ring with explicit
fence lifetimes. No per-frame path allocates without bound, waits on a worker,
or reads back the indirect buffer.
@end itemize

@section Presets

@table @strong
@item Classic
Nearest filtering, original palette and colormap rules, documented fullbright
handling, software-style sky and water warp, sprites, particles, view blends,
and an optional low internal resolution. Compared with the software reference
renderer in CI.
@item Enhanced
Linear filtering, mipmaps, anisotropy where available, MSAA, interpolated
models and full-precision lighting. Enhanced presentation never shows entities
that the original visibility rules would hide.
@end table

Neither preset changes simulation traces. Benchmark records include every
preset setting.

@section Device loss

On device loss the CPU state is kept and local play pauses. The renderer makes
one bounded rebuild and re-uploads from retained CPU data. If that fails, the
player is offered Tier 0 or save-and-exit. Recovery from a driver hang is best
effort; no claim is made that every GPU fault is recoverable.

@node Platforms
@chapter Platforms

@section Desktop

@itemize
@item
Per-user data follows each platform's convention: XDG directories on Linux,
Known Folders on Windows, Application Support on macOS. Saves, configs,
demos, screenshots, traces and logs go there. Game data directories are
mounted read-only and never written.
@item
Game data is found through @code{-basedir}, @code{QUAKE_BASEDIR}, the
platform data directories, then the current directory. If nothing is found,
the no-data screen explains where to put legally obtained files and offers a
directory picker. Tharsis never downloads game data.
@item
Linux ships through Guix, Nix and Arch packages (§5.5). Windows and macOS
ship as release archives with checksums.
@end itemize

@section Browser

@itemize
@item
The engine compiles to @code{wasm32-unknown-unknown}. The page runs the client
and renderer on the main thread; the server runs in a Web Worker and speaks
protocol 15 over @code{postMessage}.
@item
The bundle needs no @code{SharedArrayBuffer} and therefore no cross-origin
isolation headers. Work that is parallel on desktop runs serially in the
browser; this trade-off is documented here per §3.2.
@item
The player picks PAK files or a game folder. Copies are kept only in the
browser's own storage (Origin Private File System or IndexedDB) on the
player's machine. Saves, configs and demos live there too, with export and
import. Nothing is sent over the network.
@item
The bundle is a release download (NG-4). Players serve it from
@code{localhost}, which browsers treat as a secure context, so WebGPU is
available. The release notes give a one-line way to serve it.
@item
All fonts, scripts and shaders ship inside the bundle; nothing is fetched
from a third-party host (§9.1).
@item
If WebGPU is unavailable, the page falls back to Tier 0 on a canvas, or
explains which browsers and settings are supported.
@end itemize

@section Input and interface

@itemize
@item
Keyboard and mouse input through winit (pointer lock in the browser);
bindings set with vanilla @code{bind} commands and remappable. Gamepads are
optional.
@item
Configuration stays in vanilla @file{.cfg} form. Unknown commands and cvars
produce bounded warnings.
@item
Menus are keyboard-complete. Defaults do not capture @code{Insert},
@code{CapsLock} or assistive-technology chords.
@item
Adopted from §18.5.2 although games are exempt: remappable controls, HUD and
text scaling, independent sound and music volumes, and a reduced-flash and
reduced-motion option (view bob, damage tilt, screen flashes).
@item
The no-data screen and debug overlay use the @code{steelbore} theme and
bundled OFL fonts (§11, §12). In-game text uses the game's own font data.
@end itemize

@node Compatibility
@chapter Compatibility

@section Compatibility manifest

M0 creates a versioned manifest (R-311). It lists:

@itemize
@item
the accepted shareware and registered data editions, with release, origin and
SHA-256 of every PAK;
@item
the qualified LibreQuake release and subset, with its licence audit;
@item
the community corpus — @emph{Arcane Dimensions}, @emph{Copper} and
@emph{Alkaline} — with versions, hashes, and the formats, protocols,
extensions and limits each needs;
@item
the limits table: entities, edicts, models, sounds, lightmaps, visible
surfaces, message sizes, file sizes, VM memory, recursion depth, each a bounded
number with a diagnostic when exceeded; and
@item
for each claimed entry, the test, platform, input hash, result, known
deviations, and whether it may be used in public CI.
@end itemize

A release claims compatibility only for entries in the manifest with a passing
regression test. Licensed data and results that contain protected material stay
in the maintainer's local acceptance environment.

@section Community formats

The MVP supports what QuakeSpasm-level ports support and the M6 corpus needs:
BSP2 and 2PSB maps, protocol 666 for the loopback and demos, raised limits,
fog, skyboxes, entity alpha and @file{.lit} coloured lighting. Protocol 999 is
added only if the manifest shows the corpus needs it. Mission packs and the
2021 re-release follow in M9.

@section Bug compatibility

A documented vanilla behaviour is preserved when gameplay, speedruns, demos
or mods depend on it, even when it is a bug. A behaviour that only permits
memory corruption, reading freed memory, arbitrary file access or unbounded
computation is replaced by a checked error and recorded as an intentional
safety difference.

@node Quality
@chapter Quality

@section Fidelity contract

The oracle is the id Software GPL WinQuake source built out of tree at a
pinned commit, headless, with SSE2 and floating-point contraction disabled.
Its build flags, CPU and harness changes are recorded.

@itemize
@item
A command-driven harness feeds both engines the same ordered commands, random
seed and explicit time-step sequence. M0 freezes a constant 1/72-second
sequence and representative variable-step sequences.
@item
@strong{Exact:} entity spawn and removal order, discrete state, trigger
activation order, map transitions, QuakeC opcode results and protocol bytes.
@item
@strong{Within frozen tolerances:} positions, velocities, angles and event
times, with limits set per field in M0 and recorded with the float encoding,
reference build, seed and inputs.
@item
@strong{Bit-exact} only where a test in a qualified environment shows it. It
is not inferred from transliteration or from banning fused multiply-add,
though both practices are kept because they make it likely.
@item
Demo checks cover message decoding, rendered client state and
record-and-replay. Demos alone never discharge server requirements.
@item
A difference from the oracle in the default fixed-step mode needs a
maintainer-approved exception and a regression fixture before release. A
known difference never counts as a passing parity test.
@end itemize

@section Visual checks

@itemize
@item
Software reference frames: exact palette-index checks against oracle captures
for frozen scenes covering opaque geometry, light styles, dynamic lights,
water and sky, models and sprites, particles and the HUD. Resolution,
viewport, gamma, filtering and interpolation are fixed per capture.
@item
Classic GPU frames against software references: starting thresholds of SSIM
at least 0.99 and at most 0.5 percent of pixels off by more than 8/255 per
channel. After the first working renderer, per-scene thresholds and masks
are measured and frozen by the maintainer. HUD text and visibility keep exact
region checks.
@item
Enhanced mode and intentional rasterisation differences use separately
approved GPU golden images, each recording its adapter, driver and rationale.
@item
Images derived from licensed content stay in the local environment.
@end itemize

@section Performance targets

These are proposed engineering targets, not measurements. M0 names the
machines and freezes CPU, GPU, RAM, OS, driver, browser, power mode and display
settings (R-313). Runs are at least five 120-second runs after a 30-second
warm-up, reporting median, 95th, 99th percentile and worst frame interval,
CPU time, GPU timestamps where available, and allocations. Timestamps that are
unavailable are reported as unavailable, never as zero.

@multitable @columnfractions .3 .4 .3
@headitem Measure @tab Workload @tab Target
@item Frame interval, integrated GPU
@tab 1920×1080, Tier 1 Classic, original maps
@tab Median ≤ 8.33 ms; p99 ≤ 16.67 ms
@item Frame interval, discrete GPU
@tab 2560×1440, Tier 2 Enhanced, original maps
@tab Median ≤ 4.17 ms; p99 ≤ 8.33 ms
@item Frame interval, discrete GPU
@tab 2560×1440, Tier 2 Enhanced, largest corpus map
@tab Median ≤ 8.33 ms; p99 ≤ 16.67 ms
@item Frame interval, browser
@tab 1920×1080, Tier 1 Classic, original maps, integrated GPU
@tab Median ≤ 16.67 ms
@item Windows and macOS
@tab Same workloads on their reference machines
@tab Targets of the matching GPU class
@item Server step
@tab Busiest approved fixture
@tab p99 ≤ 1 ms original; ≤ 2 ms corpus
@item CPU submission
@tab Tier 2, discrete profile
@tab p95 ≤ 1 ms
@item Map load
@tab Largest map, local SSD, warm cache
@tab ≤ 1 s original; ≤ 3 s corpus; cold load reported
@item Memory
@tab Steady play
@tab RSS ≤ 512 MiB original, ≤ 1.5 GiB corpus; GPU ≤ 512 MiB original
@item Browser bundle
@tab WASM, script and shaders, compressed
@tab ≤ 15 MiB
@item Soak
@tab Four hours of demos and map transitions
@tab No crash; RSS growth after warm-up ≤ 5%
@end multitable

A target changes only with a dated justification, old and new values,
measurements and maintainer approval; release evidence reports both.

@section Untrusted input and failure handling

@itemize
@item
Files, decoded counts, recursion, VM instructions, config expansion and
allocations are bounded. Lengths, offsets and indices are checked before use.
Malformed input never reaches @code{unwrap}, @code{expect} or panicking
indexing.
@item
Content mounts are read-only. Absolute paths, traversal and symlink escapes
are rejected. Native plugins and replacement shaders are out of scope.
@item
Every loader, the VM, demos, saves and config commands have fuzz targets with
a minimised permanent regression corpus. A release has no open, reproducible
crash in that corpus.
@item
Writes use a temporary file and atomic replace; a failed write keeps the last
valid save. No audio means silence with one warning; no GPU means Tier 0; no
data means the no-data screen.
@end itemize

@section Privacy

No analytics, crash upload, accounts, tracking or advertising. Logs and traces
stay local. Data discovery checks documented locations only. Test-data
fetching is opt-in, pinned, and never part of a player build.

@section Licensing and identity

The upstream source carries GPL-2.0-or-later grants; new engine code is
GPL-3.0-or-later and upstream notices are kept verbatim with per-file
provenance. Generated shader artifacts, fixtures and third-party content each
carry REUSE provenance. Game data is separate: the GPL release keeps its data
restrictions, so nothing is bundled, mirrored or cached publicly without
confirmed permission. id Software and substantial third-party work are
credited in @file{CREDITS.md}.

